⏺ Wrote it to tmp/epicycles_generalization.md. The short version:

  The generalization

  Every curve in all four files is one family — a sum of uniform circular 
  motions (an epicycle / truncated Fourier series):

  $$z(t) = \sum_{k=0}^{N-1} r_k, e^{i(\omega_k t + \phi_k)}$$

  The HTML files are the special case $N=3$, all phases $\phi_k=0$ (why every
  figure starts pointing due east), with radius/freq → $r_k$/$\omega_k$.

  What this family contains

  - $N=1$: circle. $N=2$: all Spirograph curves — epitrochoid (same direction)
    vs hypotrochoid (opposite), with cardioid, nephroid, astroid, deltoid as
    special ratios; roses when radii are equal and signs opposite. $N\to\infty$:
    Fourier synthesis — any closed curve is an epicycle.
  - Closure: the figure closes iff all frequency ratios are rational. With
    $\omega_k = n_k\Omega$, period $T = 2\pi/\Omega$, and $\gcd(n_k)$-fold
    rotational symmetry. If a dominant arm $r_0 > \sum r_k$ exists, the winding
    number around the center is $n_0$.

  What each file actually draws

  ┌──────────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────┐
  │                 File                 │            Closed iff             │
  ├──────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────┤
  │                                      │ $k \mid f_1f_2$ (and note:        │
  │ triple_rotating_circle_animated.html │ radius2 is overwritten to 0, so   │
  │                                      │ it's really a 2-arm curve)        │
  ├──────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────┤
  │ ...rotation_r1_r2_index.html         │ never — the $1/3$ arm makes 33⅓   │
  │                                      │ turns                             │
  ├──────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────┤
  │ ...savefile_index.html               │ $k \in {2,4,5,10}$ (100 turns,    │
  │                                      │ $\omega_0=1/k$)                   │
  ├──────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────┤
  │ ...savefile_uniq_index.html          │ $k \mid f_1f_2$                   │
  └──────────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────┘

  The $2\pi f_1 f_2$ sweep bound is clever — it guarantees the inner arms close
  exactly; only the slow $1/k$ drift arm can leave a "seam".

  Directions of generalization

  Nonzero phases, negative frequencies (clockwise arms → roses/hypocycloids),
  damping ($r_k e^{-\lambda_k t}$ → harmonograph), frequency chirp, per-axis
  Lissajous amplitudes, a third dimension, and Chebyshev form (integer
  frequencies make the curve algebraic). The doc ends with a single
  drawEpicycle(canvas, arms, sweeps) function that reproduces all four pages —
  each page is just a different arms array and sweep count.

